Bugfix: Revoked access ends the session of a sync client

Sync clients send their session cookie along with every request. After an app
password was revoked in the personal settings, a client that used it kept
syncing: requests with the revoked app password and the cookie, and requests
with the cookie alone, were answered with 207. Sessions started by a client
itself (WebDAV, HTTP Basic, OAuth2) were never checked against the credentials
they were started with, and every request extended them. Removing such a
session under "Sessions" in the settings had no effect either, its session
token was simply created again with the next request. A session started with
an OAuth2 token went on accepting requests with the cookie alone after the
desktop authorization was revoked, and a password change did not end any of
these sessions.

These sessions are now checked before every request, like browser sessions:

- App password: the session ends with the next request after the app password
  was revoked, also when the client only sends the cookie. Every
  core/last_check_timeout minutes (default 5) the password the app password
  was created with is checked, as a login with the app password does; after a
  password change the app password becomes invalid and the session ends.
  Accounts from LDAP are exempt, as on login.
- Password: when the client sends only the cookie, the password stored with
  the session is checked every core/last_check_timeout minutes; after a
  password change the session ends. Requests that send the password are
  checked by the HTTP Basic login as before.
- Session removed in the settings: the session ends with the next request. A
  client with valid credentials signs in again and gets a new session.
- OAuth2 and OpenID Connect: every request has to carry an Authorization
  header that the authentication module confirms for the user of the session.
  A different header, or none at all, is checked by the module at once, the
  same header again after core/last_check_timeout minutes. Without a token or
  with the token of another user the session ends. If the module rejects the
  token, for example because it has expired or was revoked, the request fails
  as before (401); on WebDAV the session stays for the renewed token, OCS and
  the web interface end it as before. After a revocation the session can no
  longer be used.
- The token endpoint of the OAuth2 app and the OpenID Connect discovery, which
  the desktop client calls with its cookie but without its token, leave the
  session alone. Renewing a token no longer ends the session.

A session that is no longer valid is logged out and its session token is
deleted; the request continues without a login and gets 401 unless it carries
valid credentials. Requests that send only the cookie of a session started with
an OAuth2 token are no longer accepted; the desktop and mobile clients send
their token with every request. Sessions started before the update are taken
over with their first request; if that request sends only the cookie and the
session was not started with an app password, the client signs in once more.

The check reads the session token from the database as before, for app
passwords also the app password token, which the HTTP Basic login of the same
request loads anyway. The session and the token are written only when
something changes, the last activity of the token at most once a minute. Of
parallel requests with the same token only one checks a stored password; it
takes over the check with a single conditional update. Browser sessions, public
links, guests and the early release of the session lock for sync clients are
unchanged.
